CY  - Cham
AB  - Businesses use retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) to provide output from their models, based on a credible source. Still, the same method is used to produce “high-leverage” pathways to inject prompts and leak data. This review frames RAG security as a control-plane issue: Untrusted input (retrieved text, tool output) is combined with trusted input (privileged prompt), which may direct the model’s behaviour and execute side-effecting tools. We will frame attacks as pivoting through three mechanisms: Context Manipulation, Boundary Crossing, Egress and demonstrate how each mechanism allows an attacker to bypass policy or disclose information. Rather than a single defense, we outline a layered baseline that combines source governance, safety-aware retrieval, prompt compartmentalization, scoped tool capabilities, and egress policies. We also highlight where current evaluations fall short: limited modelling of indirect injections seeded in corpora, weak measurement of exfiltration, and minimal coverage of realistic tool chains. The result is a compact map of failure modes and mitigations that practitioners can apply today while providing researchers with targets for stronger, comparable benchmarks.
